  "title": "CHAPTER 5 : GRAVITATION",
  "order": 98,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "ljmklx4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Universal Law of Gravitation states that every object attracts every other object with a force proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.",
        "Mass is an intrinsic scalar quantity that remains constant everywhere, whereas Weight (W = mg) is a vector force that varies with local gravity.",
        "Acceleration due to gravity (g) is maximum at the Earth's poles, minimum at the equator, and zero at the center of the Earth.",
        "Archimedes' Principle states that a body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced.",
        "Thrust is the total force acting perpendicularly on a surface, while pressure is thrust acting per unit area."
      ],
      "id": "97ptjfg"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Gravitational Constant (G)",
          "value": "6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²",
